Based on the provided code and the error messages, it seems that the `ReplicaSyncSession` class and its methods are not accessible due to their protection level. This is likely because the class and methods are declared as `internal` or `private`, which means they can only be accessed within the same assembly or class, respectively.

To fix this issue, you can try to make the `ReplicaSyncSession` class and its methods `public`, or you can use the `InternalsVisibleTo` attribute to make the internal members visible to the test assembly.

Alternatively, you can use a testing library such as Moq to create a mock instance of the `ReplicaSyncSession` class and test its behavior indirectly.

Here is an example of how you can modify the test code to use Moq:
```csharp
using Xunit;
using Moq;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Garnet.cluster;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

public class ReplicaSyncSessionTests
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task WaitForFlushAsync_LogsErrorOnException()
    {
        // Arrange
        var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger>();
        var replicaSyncSessionMock = new Mock<ReplicaSyncSession>(loggerMock.Object);
        replicaSyncSessionMock.Setup(r => r.WaitForFlushAsync()).Throws(new Exception("Test exception"));

        // Act
        await replicaSyncSessionMock.Object.WaitForFlushAsync();

        // Assert
        loggerMock.Verify(l => l.LogError(It.IsAny<Exception>(), "{method}", "WaitForFlushAsync"), Times.Once);
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task WaitForSyncCompletionAsync_LogsErrorOnException()
    {
        // Arrange
        var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger>();
        var replicaSyncSessionMock = new Mock<ReplicaSyncSession>(loggerMock.Object);
        replicaSyncSessionMock.Setup(r => r.WaitForSyncCompletionAsync()).Throws(new OperationCanceledException());

        // Act and Assert
        await Assert.ThrowsAsync<OperationCanceledException>(() => replicaSyncSessionMock.Object.WaitForSyncCompletionAsync());
        loggerMock.Verify(l => l.LogError(It.IsAny<Exception>(), "{method} failed waiting for sync", "WaitForSyncCompletionAsync"), Times.Once);
    }
}
```
Note that this code uses the `Mock` class from Moq to create a mock instance of the `ReplicaSyncSession` class, and then sets up the mock to throw an exception when the `WaitForFlushAsync` or `WaitForSyncCompletionAsync` method is called. The test then verifies that the logger is called with the correct error message.
